19:T517,## Substitutability is the contract

The **L** in SOLID, from Barbara Liskov: *if S is a subtype of T, you can replace a T with an S and the program stays correct.* Subclasses must honour the **behaviour** their base promised, not just the method signatures.

A subclass breaks LSP when it:

- **strengthens a precondition** (demands more than the parent),
- **weakens a postcondition** (delivers less), or
- **violates an invariant** the parent guaranteed.

## The famous trap: Square *is-a* Rectangle?

Mathematically yes. In code, **no**. A `Rectangle` lets width and height vary independently. A `Square` must keep them equal, so `Square` overrides `setWidth` to also change the height.

```
Rectangle r = new Square();
r.setWidth(5);
r.setHeight(4);
assert r.area() == 20;   // 💥 it's 16, setHeight reset the width
```

Code written against `Rectangle` silently breaks when handed a `Square`. The `is-a` looked fine; the **behaviour** wasn't substitutable.

## The fix

Don't force the inheritance. Model both as immutable `Shape`s with their own `area()`no shared setters to break. Or ask: *"can a Square stand in for a Rectangle everywhere?"* If no, it isn't a subtype.

> LSP is what makes **polymorphism trustworthy**. Break it and every `extends` becomes a place your callers can't rely on.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-solid","activeLesson":"liskov-substitution"}],["$","$L18","liskov-substitution",{"lesson":{"id":"liskov-substitution","patternId":"lld-solid","title":"Liskov Substitution (L)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"A subclass must be usable ANYWHERE its parent is, without surprising the caller. If substituting it breaks behaviour, the inheritance is a lie.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Microsoft"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Square breaks the contract","code":["class Rectangle {","    void setWidth(int w){ this.w = w; }","    void setHeight(int h){ this.h = h; }","    int area(){ return w * h; }","}","class Square extends Rectangle {","    void setWidth(int w){ this.w = w; this.h = w; }   // ⚠ also sets height","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"A Rectangle lets width and height move independently, that's its contract.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"rect","name":"Rectangle","fields":["- w, h: int"],"methods":["+ setWidth(w)","+ setHeight(h)","+ area()"],"x":1,"y":0.2},{"id":"sq","name":"Square","methods":["+ setWidth(w) ⚠","+ setHeight(h) ⚠"],"x":1,"y":1.5,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"inh","from":"sq","to":"rect","kind":"inherit"}],"shownClassIds":["rect"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["rect"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":6,"narration":"Square extends Rectangle and, to stay square, overrides setWidth to ALSO change the height. Looks reasonable… and quietly changes the parent's behaviour.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["rect","sq"],"shownRelIds":["inh"],"activeIds":["sq"]},"vars":[["override","setWidth also sets height"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":7,"narration":"A Client holds a Rectangle reference, actually a Square. It sets width to 5…","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"actor"},{"id":"r","label":"rect : Square","tint":"red"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"client","to":"r","label":"setWidth(5)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"client","to":"r","label":"setHeight(4)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"client","to":"r","label":"area()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m4","from":"r","to":"client","label":"16  (expected 20!)","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["declared","Rectangle"],["actual","Square"]]},{"view":"sequence","narration":"…then height to 4. For a real Rectangle that's fine. But Square's setHeight keeps it square, it just reset width to 4 too.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":2,"activeIndex":1}},{"view":"sequence","narration":"area() returns 16, but the Client expected 5 × 4 = 20. The substitution silently broke correct code. That is an LSP violation.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:2:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["expected","20"],["got","16  💥"]]}]},{"label":"Fix: no false is-a","code":["interface Shape { int area(); }","class Rectangle implements Shape { Rectangle(w,h){...} int area(){ return w*h; } }","class Square    implements Shape { Square(side){...}  int area(){ return s*s; } }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Drop the false inheritance. Make Shape the contract, just area().","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"shape","name":"Shape","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ area(): int"],"x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"rect","name":"Rectangle","fields":["- w, h"],"methods":["+ area()"],"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"sq","name":"Square","fields":["- side"],"methods":["+ area()"],"x":1.6,"y":1.6}],"relations":[{"id":"r1","from":"rect","to":"shape","kind":"implement"},{"id":"r2","from":"sq","to":"shape","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["shape"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["shape"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Rectangle and Square each implement Shape independently, immutable (dimensions set once in the constructor). No inherited setters to corrupt.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["shape","rect","sq"],"shownRelIds":["r1","r2"],"activeIds":["rect","sq"]}},{"view":"class","narration":"Now ANY Shape is safely substitutable, area() always means what the caller expects. Substitutability restored; polymorphism is trustworthy again.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["shape","rect","sq"],"shownRelIds":["r1","r2"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["substitutable","✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
